# HARUTA — expansion: six chapters between the route and the final choice, plus two endings.
# Running motifs carried over from 13_haruta.s25: "anything is fine", the follow-up question,
# the torn flyer / film club, the corner-store egg sandwich, the scenes in his notes app,
# staying for the credits, special thanks "after the catering".
# New flags: harutax_again, harutax_coat, harutax_read, harutax_said.
# Haruta is already in casual speech here (his room is done).

=== haruta_ch1
@autosave
@bg studio_lobby t=fade d=1.2
@music studio
@time 03:10
@amb room
@cam wide
> The lobby, side by side, as promised. Nobody applauds. Songha is standing on the second stair with his notebook open, which is his version of applause.
@show songha neutral at=l
songha: Good. You're here. First, nobody panic. Then: closing duties.
@show haruta smile at=c
haruta: Closing duties?
songha: She asked us to turn off the lights when we go. I'm interpreting that broadly.
songha: We leave this building the way we found it. Better, where possible. I've made a list.
> He turns the notebook round. It is a real list. It has boxes to tick. Two of them are already ticked, in a different pen.
@show hanbi smile at=r
hanbi: …I did the mugs.
songha[smile]: Hanbi did the mugs. Before I wrote "mugs." This is why I wrote it down and ticked it anyway.
songha: Haruta. The lounge. One projector, borrowed, currently sitting on a tower of chairs like a cat that got up a tree.
haruta[smile]: That's a fair description.
songha: It goes back where it came from. With its case, its cap, and its cord. Take {{playerName}}. It's a two-person job.
* [playful] "Is it a two-person job?"
    @rel haruta closeness+1
    songha: It is now. I've written two names.
    > He has. Yours is spelled correctly. You didn't see him ask anyone.
* [sincere] "We'll take care of it."
    @rel haruta trust+1
    songha[smile]: I know. That's why you got the one with moving parts.
* [quiet] Look at Haruta, and let him answer for both of you.
    @rel haruta trust+1
    > It takes him a second to realise the question has been handed to him. Then he stands a little straighter.
    haruta: We'll do it. Properly. I'd like to be the one who puts it back.
    songha[smile]: …Noted. In pen.
hanbi: The cord's mine. {p} Leave it on the desk after.
> Hanbi hands Haruta a phone torch that is already switched on. Then a second one, for you. It's not clear where the second one came from.
@hide songha hanbi
@sfx step
@bg studio_lounge t=fade d=1.2
@music quiet
> The lounge. The projector is cooling on its tower, ticking now and then like a kettle remembering.
> Haruta looks at it for a moment with his hands behind his back. The museum posture. Then he takes his hands out, deliberately, and puts one on the machine.
haruta: It's still warm.
haruta[thinking]: I've never put away something I got out myself. Not something like this. Usually I notice other people's things and carry those.
you: What's different about this one?
haruta[smile]: I went looking for it. Nobody suggested it. If I hadn't, it would have stayed in the cupboard until the builders came.
haruta: So it's mine to put back. That feels like the second half of wanting something. The less famous half.
> The case is not in the lounge. Neither is the lens cap. He checks under the chairs, politely, as if they might be embarrassed to be found.
haruta: The cupboard under the stairs. I took the projector and left everything else. I was in a hurry. I'm never in a hurry.
* [playful] "You robbed a cupboard. At three in the morning."
    @rel haruta closeness+1
    haruta[smile]: A very small heist. One guard. The guard was a mop.
    haruta: I apologised to it, I think. Out of habit.
* [sincere] "What were you hurrying for?"
    @rel haruta trust+1
    haruta[thinking]: I thought if I stopped to find the case, I'd stop altogether. I'd think, "It's late, it's somebody else's."
    haruta[smile]: So I carried it out before I could be reasonable. It turns out I can outrun myself over short distances.
@sfx door
> The cupboard under the stairs is the size of a confession booth and smells of floor polish. Two torches make it look like a very low-budget mystery.
> The mop is where he left it. The projector case is behind it: brown, scuffed at the corners, a luggage tag on the handle.
> And on the shelf above, under a folded dust sheet, a cardboard box. Someone has written on the side in marker: FILM CLUB. DO NOT TIDY.
haruta[thinking]: …Well. Look at that.
haruta: I didn't see that before. I was only looking for the thing I came for.
> He doesn't open it. He looks at it. Then at you. The old question is all over his face: *is this all right? would you rather not? I'm fine either way.*
> You watch him put that question down, like a bag.
haruta: I want to open it. Hold the torch?
* [quiet] Hold the torch steady, and say nothing.
    @rel haruta trust+1 romance+1
    > The light stays exactly where he needs it. He notices that it does. He doesn't say so; his shoulders say it for him.
* [playful] "It says do not tidy. It doesn't say do not open."
    @rel haruta closeness+1
    haruta[smile]: You'd make a good lawyer. Or a terrible archivist.
* [sincere] "It's been waiting for someone who'd ask."
    @rel haruta trust+1
    haruta[smile]: Then I'm glad it was me. I'm getting used to saying that.
@sfx page
> Inside: three flattened cushions, tied with string. A lens cap. A biscuit tin that rattles. And a ledger with a cloth spine, the kind schools used to give out.
> He opens the ledger on his knees. Ruled columns, in a dozen different handwritings. DATE. FILM. CHOSEN BY. CUSHIONS.
haruta: Every Friday. Look. *The Red Balloon* — chosen by Mrs Oh, guitar. *Some Like It Hot* — chosen by the twins. Eleven cushions. Fourteen.
haruta[smile]: This one just says, "chosen by accident. Wrong reel. Stayed anyway. Nine cushions."
> He turns pages the way he read the corkboard: slowly, everything, touching only the edges.
haruta[thinking]: I told you in the lobby that someone else always chose. I said it sounded relaxing.
haruta: I had it wrong. Nobody here was relaxing. Every week a different person had to stand up and say, "this one. I want you all to see this one."
haruta: They took turns being brave. They wrote it down so everyone would have to.
> The last filled row is four years old. Under it, the columns go on, ruled and empty, to the bottom of the page.
@textbox echo
echo: Recipient Haruta. That ledger is not a record. It was never submitted to me.
echo: It is classified as lost property. Lost property may be claimed by anyone who asks politely. You have asked politely all night.
@textbox normal
haruta[smile]: Thank you, ECHO. That's the first time it's been useful.
> He takes the pencil from the ledger's spine. It's on a string. Someone thought of that, years ago, so that nobody could say they had nothing to write with.
> Under DATE, he writes next Friday's. Under FILM, he stops.
haruta[thinking]: I haven't chosen yet. I said I would. I don't have it.
haruta: Before tonight I'd have written "anything" and closed the book.
* [sincere] "Leave it blank. It isn't a lie if it's blank."
    @rel haruta trust+1
    haruta[smile]: Blank isn't "anything." Blank is "not yet." You taught me those were different.
* [playful] "Write 'to be announced.' Very professional."
    @rel haruta closeness+1
    haruta[smile]: T.B.A. The three most confident letters in cinema.
    > He writes them, small and neat, and underlines them once, like a man signing a lease.
* [romantic] "Fill in the column you're sure of." {if haruta.trust >= 3}
    @rel haruta romance+1 trust+1
    > He looks at you, then at the page. He skips FILM entirely.
    haruta[smile]: Out of order. {p} I can do that.
    you: It's your book.
> Under CHOSEN BY, he writes his name. Katakana first, from habit. Then again beside it in Korean, so the book can read it.
> It takes four seconds. He looks at it for a good deal longer than four seconds.
haruta[thinking]: I've signed a lot of things. Attendance sheets. Forms. I don't think I've ever signed for wanting something.
haruta: It looks strange. Like seeing my name on a poster.
> He turns the ledger towards you and holds out the pencil on its string. The last column is still empty. CUSHIONS.
haruta: That one isn't mine to fill in. I can't decide that for anyone. I've checked.
> You write a *1*. It's a small number. It sits in its column like the first person into an empty cinema.
haruta[smile]: One cushion. We've had worse Fridays. There's one in here with zero and a drawing of a sad face.
> He closes the ledger around the pencil and puts it inside his cardigan, against his shirt, the way people carry things they haven't finished being surprised by.
> The case under one arm. The lens cap in his pocket. And the biscuit tin, which rattles again when he lifts it — not like biscuits.
haruta[thinking]: Hm. That's not a biscuit sound.
haruta[smile]: Bring the torch. I want to see what the film club thought was worth a tin.
@hide haruta
@goto haruta_ch2

=== haruta_ch2
@autosave
@bg studio_lounge t=fade d=1
@music fun
@time 03:28
@amb room
@show haruta thinking at=c
> The biscuit tin goes on the lounge table, under both torches, like something about to be operated on.
@video vid_haruta_drama
@unlock cg:cg_haruta_drama
> Inside: a reel of film no bigger than a saucer, and a label in the same marker as the box. HOUSE REEL. PLAY FIRST. EVERY TIME.
haruta[smile]: They had a house reel.
haruta: Proper cinemas did that. A little film before the film. "Please don't smoke." "Visit our kiosk." The Dalbit had one about not putting your feet on the seats.
haruta[smile]: I loved it more than some of the films.
> He holds the reel up to the torch. Tiny frames. Something pale in each one. Too small to read.
haruta: Forty seconds, maybe. {p} I want to see it.
> No *if that's all right*. No *only if there's time*. It comes out whole, and he hears it come out, and lets it stand.
* [playful] "That's three wants in twenty minutes. You're on a streak."
    @rel haruta closeness+1
    haruta[smile]: Don't count. If you count, I'll start editing myself.
* [sincere] "Then let's see it."
    @rel haruta trust+1
    haruta[smile]: Just like that. I keep expecting a form to fill in.
* [careful] "Do you know how to thread one of these?"
    @rel haruta trust+1
    haruta: No.
    haruta[smile]: I've watched about a thousand films, and I have never once been the one in the booth. I'm all audience.
> The projector's case has instructions glued inside the lid. A diagram, numbered one to seven, with a confident little arrow at every step.
> Step four has been torn off. Where it was, someone has written in biro: *you'll work it out. — Mrs Oh.*
haruta[thinking]: Mrs Oh believes in us.
you: Mrs Oh has never met us.
haruta: That's when it's easiest to believe in people.
> He pushes his sleeves up again. You hold the diagram. It is, Songha would be pleased to know, a two-person job after all.
haruta: One. Reel on the front arm. Done. Two — film comes off the top. Or the bottom. Which way is the arrow pointing?
* [playful] "Up. Definitely up. Probably."
    @rel haruta closeness+1
    haruta[smile]: "Definitely, probably." I'm putting that on the poster.
* [sincere] "I can't tell. Try it, and we'll find out."
    @rel haruta trust+1
    haruta[smile]: Try it and find out. Nobody has ever given me that as an instruction. I like it.
> Three. Five. Six. At four, he simply looks at the gap in the diagram, looks at the machine, and makes a loop with his finger that seems to satisfy them both.
@sfx projector
> The rectangle on the wall stops being empty.
> A countdown, scratched into the film by hand: 5, 4, a 3 that is clearly a 3, a 2 that is facing the wrong way —
> — and then the lounge. This lounge. Upside down. A cushion is walking across the ceiling, backwards, away from a chair.
haruta[thinking]: …
haruta: It's upside down.
you: And backwards.
haruta: And backwards.
> The cushion reverses with great dignity off the top of the wall. A title card arrives, mirror-written and standing on its head. Then white.
> Haruta laughs. Not the polite one, the one that fits into a conversation. This one is low and unguarded and goes on longer than he means it to, and he rests a hand on the chair tower until it's done.
haruta[laugh]: It left. The cushion walked out on us. That's the bleakest forty seconds I've ever seen.
@sfx step
@show daniel smile at=r
daniel: That was Haruta laughing. Properly. I came to see what earns that.
@show charlie smile at=l
charlie: There's a film running and nobody sent for me. I'm choosing not to take it personally.
haruta[smile]: There's a film. It's forty seconds. I've ruined it.
daniel[smile]: Then I have to see it. Ruined is my favourite genre.
> Rewinding takes two people and one false start. Daniel offers advice the whole time. None of it is about projectors.
@sfx projector
> Upside down. Backwards. The cushion departs.
daniel: So it's about a cushion who has had enough. I respect that.
charlie[smile]: Or he got all the way to the chair and thought better of it. I've left parties like that.
daniel: Look at the walk, though. That's a resignation. He's already cleared his desk.
haruta[thinking]: …I think he went to get the other cushions.
> They both turn to look at him.
haruta: He got to the chair and saw it was only one chair. So he went back for everyone. You just can't see it, because the film ends.
charlie[smile]: …That's better than either of ours. Annoyingly.
daniel[smile]: Because it's Haruta. He'd give a cushion a reason.
daniel: Right way up next time, director. We've got a claw machine to say goodbye to. Charlie's going to make a speech.
charlie[smile]: A few words. {p} There's a difference, and I intend to observe it.
@hide daniel charlie
> They go. Their voices go on down the corridor, arguing about whether a cushion can hand in its notice.
> Haruta rewinds alone this time. His hands already know where step four goes.
haruta: Flip the reel. Feed from the other side. {p} There. I think Mrs Oh would nod.
@sfx projector
@music quiet
> 5, 4, 3, 2.
> The lounge, years ago, the right way up. The same sofas, less faded. A cushion inches across the carpet one frame at a time, in the patient jerk of stop-motion.
> It reaches a chair. It hops onto the seat. Behind it — from under the sofas, off the shelves, out of a guitar case — come more cushions. Eleven. Fourteen.
> Then a card, hand-lettered, held up to the camera by two hands in a cardigan not unlike his: PLEASE SIT DOWN. WE ARE ABOUT TO BEGIN.
> White. Tick, tick, tick.
haruta[smile]: …He did go back for everyone.
you: You guessed the ending.
haruta: I didn't guess. I wrote the one I wanted, and then it happened to be there.
haruta[smile]: That almost never works. Don't tell anyone it worked.
> He stands with his hand on the warm lamp housing, looking at the blank wall as if the cushions might come back for a bow.
haruta: {{playerName}}. Again?
> One word. Not *would you mind*. Not *we should probably go*. He asks it the way you'd ask for more of something at a table where you know you're welcome.
* [sincere] "Again."
    @rel haruta trust+1 closeness+1
    @flag harutax_again
    haruta[smile]: You didn't even look at the time.
    you: Neither did you.
* [playful] "Again. But I'm reviewing it properly this time."
    @rel haruta closeness+1
    @flag harutax_again
    haruta[smile]: Be fair. It's a debut work. The lead is a cushion.
    you: The lead is very good.
    haruta: The lead is *extraordinary.*
* [romantic] "As many times as you like. I like watching you watch it." {if haruta.closeness >= 3}
    @rel haruta romance+1 closeness+1
    @flag harutax_again
    > He holds your eyes a moment longer than the line needs. He used to look away. He doesn't now.
    haruta[smile]: …You sit in the back row of everything, don't you. Even when you're standing next to me.
    haruta: I like it. I'm saying so.
@sfx projector
> So you watch it again. And once more, because the third time you both notice the cat asleep on the windowsill in the background, entirely unmoved by cinema.
> He says the title card along with it the third time, under his breath. *Please sit down. We are about to begin.*
haruta: I know what plays first on Friday.
haruta[smile]: I still don't know what plays second. But first is handled. First is forty seconds and a cat.
> He winds the little reel back into its tin and presses the lid down with both thumbs. He doesn't put it in the box marked DO NOT TIDY.
> It goes into his cardigan pocket, on the other side from the ledger. He is starting to be lopsided with things he's decided to keep.
@textbox echo
echo: For accuracy: I have no record of that film. It was never submitted.
echo: I watched it three times just now. I would like to note that the cat does not move in any of them. I checked each time.
@textbox normal
haruta[smile]: Special thanks: the cat.
haruta: Large font. Before the catering.
@hide haruta
@goto haruta_ch3

=== haruta_ch3
@autosave
@bg studio_frontdesk t=fade d=1.2
@music studio
@time 03:46
@amb room
@cam wide
> The front desk. Hanbi's extension cord, coiled the way he coils it, goes on the counter. The cupboard key goes back on its hook.
> You hang it without looking. Third hook from the left, under the one with the green tag. Your hand knew before you did.
@show haruta thinking at=c
haruta: You didn't check the numbers.
you: I used to work here. A long time ago. Weekends.
haruta[smile]: I know. It's in half the records. But knowing it and watching your hand do that aren't the same.
> He comes round the counter slowly, asking permission from the furniture. There is a stool, a drawer that sticks, a visitors' book, and a brass bell with a worn spot where the thumbs went.
haruta: What was it like? Sitting here.
* [sincere] "Quiet. I liked knowing where everything went."
    @rel haruta trust+1
    haruta[thinking]: Because nothing else stayed where it was.
    > He says it gently, not as a guess. You told him in the cinema; he kept it.
    haruta: A desk where the keys have numbers. I understand that completely.
* [playful] "Mostly I told people the toilet was upstairs."
    @rel haruta closeness+1
    haruta[smile]: Every building needs one person who knows that. It's a sacred office.
    haruta: I'd have asked you three times and apologised each time.
* [quiet] Pull the sticking drawer open with the little lift-and-tug it needs.
    @rel haruta trust+1 closeness+1
    > It opens first go. Inside: rubber bands, a dead pen, a roll of raffle tickets. He watches your hand, not the drawer.
    haruta[smile]: It remembers you. Drawers don't do that for just anybody.
> He sits on the stool. It's too low for him; his knees come up. He puts both hands flat on the counter like a clerk in a very old film.
haruta: Good evening. Welcome to Studio 25. How may I—
haruta[smile]: No. I'd be hopeless at it. Someone would ask which room, and I'd say whichever they preferred.
> The roll of raffle tickets has come out of the drawer with your hand. Pink, numbered, perforated. He looks at it like a man who has just had an idea and has decided to keep it.
haruta[thinking]: I'd like to suggest a game. I don't usually. Daniel runs that department.
haruta: I ask you a this-or-that. You answer in three seconds. Then I'm allowed one follow-up question. Exactly one.
haruta: Then we swap. Every answer earns a ticket.
you: And if somebody says "anything is fine"?
haruta[smile]: Then somebody rings the bell. So the whole building knows.
> He says *somebody* with the calm of a man who knows exactly who.
haruta: I'll start easy. Rain on the window, or rain on an umbrella?
* [sincere] "On the window. From inside, with nowhere to be."
    @rel haruta trust+1
    haruta: Follow-up. Do you often have nowhere to be?
    you: Almost never. That's why.
    haruta[smile]: Ticket. That was a good one. I'm keeping the answer as well.
* [playful] "Umbrella. It's the only time rain sounds expensive."
    @rel haruta closeness+1
    haruta[smile]: Follow-up: whose umbrella?
    you: Borrowed. Never returned.
    haruta: Then you owe a man a shop visit. Handle first. Ticket.
* [quiet] "Neither. The minute after it stops."
    @rel haruta trust+1 closeness+1
    haruta[thinking]: That wasn't one of the options.
    haruta[smile]: …You're allowed. Follow-up: why that minute?
    you: Everything's still wet, and nobody's decided what to do yet.
    haruta: Two tickets. One for the answer and one for cheating beautifully.
> He tears them off along the perforation and lays them on your side of the counter, straight, like dealing a card.
you: My turn. Salt or seaweed?
haruta: Seaweed.
haruta[thinking]: …I don't even like seaweed much. That was reflex.
you: Follow-up. What do you actually like?
haruta[thinking]: Honey butter. I've never said so, because there are eight of us and one bag, and it's always gone.
haruta[smile]: Three years of wanting honey butter, and I just said "seaweed" with total confidence. That habit has deep roots.
> Ticket. He tucks it into his shirt pocket behind the film-strip pin, very seriously.
you: Subtitles or dubbed?
haruta: Subtitles. I want the real voices, even when I can't follow them. Especially then.
you: Morning or night?
haruta: Night. This one. Specifically.
you: First row or last?
haruta: Whichever you—
> He hears it. You both hear it. His hand is already halfway to the bell, like someone paying a fine he agrees with.
@sfx bell
> It is much louder than either of you expected. It goes out into the lobby and up the stairwell and comes back.
> From two floors up, faintly, Hyunjun: *Was that Haruta?* And Justin, unhurried: *That's one. I'm keeping count.*
haruta[laugh]: They don't even know the rules. They just know.
* [playful] "How did it feel, paying the fine?"
    @rel haruta closeness+1
    haruta: Fair. Honestly. It should always have cost something. It was free for too long.
* [sincere] "You were being kind. You just did it in the wrong column."
    @rel haruta trust+1 romance+1
    haruta[thinking]: The wrong column.
    haruta[smile]: That's exactly it. I put "what would please you" in the box marked "what I want." For years. The accounts don't add up.
* [quiet] Slide him a ticket anyway.
    @rel haruta trust+1
    > He looks at it. Then at you.
    haruta[smile]: For what?
    you: For ringing it yourself.
> He straightens on the stool. Rolls his shoulders, like a swimmer on a block.
haruta: Again. Faster. Don't give me time to be polite.
you: Tea or coffee?
haruta: Tea.
you: Window or aisle?
@if var.seat == "aisle"
    haruta: Window. You have the aisle. I've arranged my whole life around that in one night.
@elif var.seat == "back"
    haruta: Window. I'll describe what's outside, and you can watch the carriage. Between us we'd miss nothing.
@else
    haruta: Window. We can't both have the middle on a train. I've thought about it.
you: Long film or short?
haruta: Long. With an interval. And ice cream.
you: Stage or seats?
haruta: Stage.
> The word is out and sitting on the counter between you before he has looked at it.
> The game stops. Not awkwardly. The way a film stops when the projectionist has seen something in the frame.
haruta[surprised]: …I said stage.
haruta[thinking]: I was sure I'd say seats. I'm the one who loves being in the audience. I know where I like to sit in fifty different cinemas.
haruta: If you'd given me ten seconds, I'd have said, "both are nice." I'd have meant it, too.
> He turns the ticket roll over and over in his hands.
haruta: The slow answers are the polite ones. I hadn't noticed that. The fast ones come out before I can dress them.
you: I get a follow-up.
haruta[smile]: You do. Exactly one.
* [sincere] "What's on the stage, when you picture it?"
    @rel haruta trust+1
    haruta[thinking]: Eight of us. Lights I can't see past. And one second, right before the music, when nobody's told me what to feel yet.
    haruta: I've never said that to anyone. It sounds like a lot, out loud.
* [quiet] "I'll save it. Ask me to ask you later."
    @rel haruta trust+1 romance+1
    haruta[smile]: You can save a follow-up?
    you: House rules. I used to work here.
    haruta[smile]: …Then I'll ask you to ask. When I've found somewhere with more air.
* [playful] "Do you want the ticket, or do you want to pretend you said seats?"
    @rel haruta closeness+1
    haruta[smile]: The ticket. I said it. It's mine.
> You tear it off. He doesn't put this one in his pocket with the others. He holds it, number up, reading it like a fortune.
haruta: Two hundred and six.
haruta[smile]: I'm told the roof needs its chairs brought down. It's on Songha's list, under "mugs."
haruta: Come up with me? There's something I want to say where the ceiling's further away.
> He rings the bell once more on his way round the counter. Lightly, this time. On purpose.
haruta: That one wasn't a fine. I just like it.
@hide haruta
@goto haruta_ch4

=== haruta_ch4
@autosave
@bg studio_rooftop t=slow d=1.8
@music quiet
@time 04:04
@amb wind
@cam reveal
> The rain has stopped. The roof is one wide puddle with the city in it, and two folding chairs somebody left open, facing nothing in particular.
> Songha's list says *chairs, roof, x2*. It doesn't say when.
@show haruta neutral at=c
> Haruta tips the water off each seat, then dries them with his cardigan sleeve before you can offer yours. He sits. So do you.
> For a while, that's all. A taxi goes by, four floors down. The laundromat sign hums. The clouds are coming apart slowly, without any hurry.
haruta: It's the minute after it stops.
> He doesn't say anything else for some time. He has the pink raffle ticket in his fingers, folding one corner down and smoothing it flat again.
> It isn't the old silence, the one where he waits to find out what everyone else would like. He's getting something ready. You can see him setting it out.
* [quiet] Wait. The chairs aren't going anywhere.
    @rel haruta trust+1 romance+1
    > You look at the puddle instead of at him. It seems to be what he needs: company that isn't an audience.
    haruta: …Thank you. You're very good at not rushing a scene.
* [sincere] "There was something you wanted to say up here."
    @rel haruta trust+1
    haruta[smile]: There is. I'm finding the first line. The first line's always the hard one.
* [careful] "We can just take the chairs down, if it isn't the night for it."
    @rel haruta trust+1
    haruta[thinking]: No. It's the night for it. I checked. {p} But thank you for leaving the door open.
haruta: Ask me again. The one from the desk.
you: Stage or seats?
haruta: Stage.
> Just as fast. He nods at it, the way you nod at someone you've met once and are glad to see again.
haruta[thinking]: Can I tell you what I usually say? When people ask about it. Debuting.
haruta: Teachers. Neighbours. The woman at the dry cleaner's. "Are you excited? Are you nervous? When is it?"
haruta: I say, "Whenever it happens is fine. However it turns out is fine." And I smile, and they say, "What a calm young man."
