> The others have gone ahead to the corner store. He asked them to. Out loud, in front of everyone: *we'll catch up — I want ten minutes.* Nobody made a joke. Daniel nodded once, like a man witnessing a signature.
> He walks you two streets over instead, to a shuttered key-cutting shop with a narrow staircase beside it. A dark sign. A schedule taped inside the glass.
@show haruta smile at=c
haruta: The Dalbit. The real one. It's been eight minutes away all night.
> He stands in front of it with his hands at his sides. Not in his pockets. You know what that means now.
haruta[thinking]: I'm going to say something, and then I'm going to say a second thing. Please wait for the second one. They go together.
haruta: I like you. {p} Not the way I like a regular, or a good seat. I like you the way I wanted honey butter. For a long time, specifically, and I kept saying something else.
haruta[smile]: That's the first thing. It's true. I checked it on a roof.
* [sincere] "I like you too. Specifically."
    @rel haruta romance+1
    haruta[shy]: …Specifically. {p} All right. I needed a second. I've had it.
* [quiet] Take your hand out of your own pocket, and hold it out. Let him see it's the same answer.
    @rel haruta romance+1 trust+1
    > He looks at it. He puts his hand in yours, briefly, completely, like signing something. Then he gives it back so he can finish.
    haruta[smile]: I thought so. I hoped so. I'd like to hear it as well, some time. Not now. I'm mid-speech.
    you: I like you too, Haruta.
    haruta: …Now works. {p} Thank you.
haruta: Here's the second thing. I learned to want things about four hours ago. I'm still new at it. Tonight I ordered an egg sandwich, and my hands weren't steady.
haruta: I don't want you to be what I practise on. I want to be good at it by the time it's you.
> It isn't an apology. He says it like a director asking for one more week before the opening, because the film deserves it.
you: I have a second thing too.
you: I've never stayed anywhere past spring. I want to find out that I can. For myself, first. Before I promise it to somebody I like this much.
haruta[smile]: Then we're asking for the same thing. We just came at it from opposite aisles.
haruta: Not "no." Not "later, maybe." A date. With a date on it.
> He turns to the schedule in the glass and runs his finger down the column, reading slowly, as he did three years ago. He reads it easily now.
haruta: The first Friday of March. The late screening. Ten past eleven. Row F.
haruta: You always left in spring. I'd like to be standing here when it arrives, and see you coming up the street.
* [sincere] "First Friday of March. I'll be here."
    @rel haruta trust+1
    haruta[smile]: I know. That's the strange part. I'm not even nervous about it. I'm only nervous about what to wear.
* [playful] "Who chooses the film?"
    @rel haruta closeness+1
    haruta: I do. It'll be terrible. You bring the cushion.
    haruta[smile]: They have seats, I know. Bring it anyway. It's the principle.
* [romantic] "And between now and March?"
    @rel haruta romance+1
    haruta[smile]: Every Friday. Film club. I'll sit next to you, and I won't pretend I've forgotten this, and neither will you.
    haruta: We'll just let it come at its own speed. Like credits. You can't make them go faster. I've tried.
> He takes out his half of the lavender flyer. You find yours, in the pocket where it has been all night. FRIDAY FILM on one. CLUB. BRING A CUSHION on the other.
> He turns yours over and writes on the back with the ledger's pencil, leaning on the Dalbit's door. The date. 23:10. *Row F.*
> Then he holds out his own half, blank side up, and the pencil.
haruta: You write mine. So we each have it in the other one's handwriting. Neither of us can say we misread.
> You write it. The date. The time. After a moment, underneath: *I'm not going anywhere. Take your time.*
haruta[smile]: That's my favourite line. {p} You knew that.
> He holds his half up. You hold up yours. The torn edges meet, not straight, the way a ticket is torn at the door, and for a second it is one flyer again.
> Then you each take back your own. The smaller half. The part they give you to keep.
haruta[smile]: Breakfast? I've got an item at ten past six, and a whole bag of something I'm not sharing.
haruta: Except with you. In March, and also now.
> The sun comes up on a shuttered cinema with a schedule taped inside its window. You walk to the corner store side by side.
> Neither of you hurries. It's a long film, and you've only seen the first reel.
@ending haruta_someday
